(2369371, 324692, 3032, '<td valign="top">I really liked the classic b&amp;w feel and the crop of this and gave it an 8. <br/></td>', 'I really liked the classic b&w feel and the crop of this and gave it an 8. ', '2006-05-01 18:18:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369395, 325121, 11854, '<td valign="top">I see what you were going for...and I do see a man''s reflection at the bottom corner, yet, it seems to be out of focus a bit. I didn''t vote on this but probably would have been a 4. I''m sorry, but I really can''t think of anything to improve the shot other than more focus.</td>', 'I see what you were going for...and I do see a man''s reflection at the bottom corner, yet, it seems to be out of focus a bit. I didn''t vote on this but probably would have been a 4. I''m sorry, but I really can''t think of anything to improve the shot other than more focus.', '2006-05-01 18:28:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369400, 324862, 11854, '<td valign="top">I like the window itself, but probably would have cropped the bottom split frame as the view through there is very distracting. As it is, I probably would have voted this a 5 or 6, cropped would have been a 7.</td>', 'I like the window itself, but probably would have cropped the bottom split frame as the view through there is very distracting. As it is, I probably would have voted this a 5 or 6, cropped would have been a 7.', '2006-05-01 18:31:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369438, 325121, 36065, '<td valign="top">--Trading Post Comment--<br/>This is just noy a very DPC friendly shot.  They (we) need vibrant colors and lots of neat image to vote high on this site.  The shot as a whole is not bad, but nothing really jumps out as an appealing subject.  It seems that the most focused oart of the image is the wooden part of the window, thus pulling the viewer''s eye to that spot with little interest.  The composition is fine, but the shot just lacks some interest.  I do not believe that titles make or break an image, but again, this is DPC.  OFten we need to be told waht we need to think about an image.  I rarely even read the title before I comment or vote for the shot.</td>', '--Trading Post Comment--This is just noy a very DPC friendly shot.  They (we) need vibrant colors and lots of neat image to vote high on this site.  The shot as a whole is not bad, but nothing really jumps out as an appealing subject.  It seems that the most focused oart of the image is the wooden part of the window, thus pulling the viewer''s eye to that spot with little interest.  The composition is fine, but the shot just lacks some interest.  I do not believe that titles make or break an image, but again, this is DPC.  OFten we need to be told waht we need to think about an image.  I rarely even read the title before I comment or vote for the shot.', '2006-05-01 18:44:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369454, 324862, 36065, '<td valign="top">--Trading Post Comment--<br/>You captured the vibrant colors needed for high votes on here, but the subject is too busy for my taste.  The color and clarity are fabulous, but there is just too much to look at.  Perhaps isolating an individual pane would have made a more dramatic impact.  Nearly 5.6, not bad for this site.</td>', '--Trading Post Comment--You captured the vibrant colors needed for high votes on here, but the subject is too busy for my taste.  The color and clarity are fabulous, but there is just too much to look at.  Perhaps isolating an individual pane would have made a more dramatic impact.  Nearly 5.6, not bad for this site.', '2006-05-01 18:49:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369476, 324862, 14414, '<td valign="top">The bright colours work really well with the black borders - certainly you did well to spot the potential. I really like the dynamics, but it does seem a bit busy a composition. The 4 different "windows" add a real interest to the scene, but haven''t been fully exploited - there don''t seem to be distinct different subjects in each frame, except the row of plants in the middle one. If they all had centred on somthing different, or some been empty to contrast with more full ones, this could have, I believe, added somewhat to the composition.<br/> <br/>I''m curious as to how the scene changed at different times of day. The light seems to be high in the sky, facing the wall directly - maybe earlier/later you may have got some more exciting lighting - maybe backlightinh on the flags, or raking light across the jutting-out-bits of the building.<br/> <br/><i>after reading thru other comments - I agree that maybe it could have been made less busy by cropping out the bottom frames. I think this could have lent interest had they bee nframing something specific, but as it is, they just add confusion</i></td>', 'The bright colours work really well with the black borders - certainly you did well to spot the potential. I really like the dynamics, but it does seem a bit busy a composition. The 4 different "windows" add a real interest to the scene, but haven''t been fully exploited - there don''t seem to be distinct different subjects in each frame, except the row of plants in the middle one. If they all had centred on somthing different, or some been empty to contrast with more full ones, this could have, I believe, added somewhat to the composition. I''m curious as to how the scene changed at different times of day. The light seems to be high in the sky, facing the wall directly - maybe earlier/later you may have got some more exciting lighting - maybe backlightinh on the flags, or raking light across the jutting-out-bits of the building. after reading thru other comments - I agree that maybe it could have been made less busy by cropping out the bottom frames. I think this could have lent interest had they bee nframing something specific, but as it is, they just add confusion', '2006-05-01 18:55:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369530, 325121, 14414, '<td valign="top">Hmmmm, I''ve tried to make it <i>say</i> something to me, but I just can''t. Seems as though it could have something if you were making some comment on consumerism or fashion, or purely harking back to Dr Who-style mannequin monsters, but with the title "Reflectively Staring at Her Man" it just seems a shot through a window that was entered just in order to enter something.<br/> <br/>The more I look at it, the more I dislike the title - it shouldn''t be an issue, but it obviously is here on DPC, and I would even have prefered it untitled.<br/> <br/>Maybe if the focus wasn''t just on her, but there was some context given, by shoppers walking by, and her gaze unchanged or something, maybe that could work...Sorry I don''t really have any suggestions for improvement, its just that the subject matter doesn''t do anything for me.</td>', 'Hmmmm, I''ve tried to make it say something to me, but I just can''t. Seems as though it could have something if you were making some comment on consumerism or fashion, or purely harking back to Dr Who-style mannequin monsters, but with the title "Reflectively Staring at Her Man" it just seems a shot through a window that was entered just in order to enter something. The more I look at it, the more I dislike the title - it shouldn''t be an issue, but it obviously is here on DPC, and I would even have prefered it untitled. Maybe if the focus wasn''t just on her, but there was some context given, by shoppers walking by, and her gaze unchanged or something, maybe that could work...Sorry I don''t really have any suggestions for improvement, its just that the subject matter doesn''t do anything for me.', '2006-05-01 19:08:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369612, 324139, 30959, '<td valign="top">What a captivating shot. It''s warmth and affection keep bringing me back to it. Her expression and posture are cherubic. A shot that excels.</td>', 'What a captivating shot. It''s warmth and affection keep bringing me back to it. Her expression and posture are cherubic. A shot that excels.', '2006-05-01 19:31:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369623, 324430, 40797, '<td valign="top">Kelly, I am so happy for you! You are a wonderful photographer. I reallly admire your work. Congratulations!!!</td>', 'Kelly, I am so happy for you! You are a wonderful photographer. I reallly admire your work. Congratulations!!!', '2006-05-01 19:36:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369651, 323620, 47117, '<td valign="top">Thank you so much Sir_bazz, and thank you all for your wonderful comments, and the fav!!!</td>', 'Thank you so much Sir_bazz, and thank you all for your wonderful comments, and the fav!!!', '2006-05-01 19:48:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369656, 323620, 47117, '<td valign="top">BobsterLobster, I didn''t mark your comment as helpful because I didn''t understand it. You said you loved the colors and wanted to be there but the composition does nothing for you. To me that is an oxymoron.<br/> <br/>To sjturner, yours wasn''t marked helpful because you simply said "I don''t see it". I can''t understand what it is you didn''t see that all the others did?<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-01 18:52:18.</i></td>', 'BobsterLobster, I didn''t mark your comment as helpful because I didn''t understand it. You said you loved the colors and wanted to be there but the composition does nothing for you. To me that is an oxymoron. To sjturner, yours wasn''t marked helpful because you simply said "I don''t see it". I can''t understand what it is you didn''t see that all the others did?', '2006-05-01 19:51:34.000000', '2006-05-01 19:52:18.000000', 0),
(2369661, 324712, 47117, '<td valign="top">I gave this a 9. I think you got ripped off. :)  Wonderful photo!! <br/> <br/>I knew this was yours though, from when you stated your high scores in the challenge-score thread. I said to myself, "it "MUST" be the India shot". When I went to your profile, sure enough, as soon as I saw you were from India, it was plain as day which shot was yours. You may want to watch that "revealing scores" thing. :)  There aren''t a ton of India entries, and this one clearly was from India, at least to me. So you could easily give away your anon. <br/> <br/>It placed well, but I think it should have placed even higher.  <br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-01 18:58:15.</i></td>', 'I gave this a 9. I think you got ripped off. :)  Wonderful photo!!  I knew this was yours though, from when you stated your high scores in the challenge-score thread. I said to myself, "it "MUST" be the India shot". When I went to your profile, sure enough, as soon as I saw you were from India, it was plain as day which shot was yours. You may want to watch that "revealing scores" thing. :)  There aren''t a ton of India entries, and this one clearly was from India, at least to me. So you could easily give away your anon.  It placed well, but I think it should have placed even higher.  ', '2006-05-01 19:53:55.000000', '2006-05-01 19:58:15.000000', 0),
(2369692, 323718, 47117, '<td valign="top">Wonderful shot. Deserved a higher placement.</td>', 'Wonderful shot. Deserved a higher placement.', '2006-05-01 20:01:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369803, 324110, 15919, '<td valign="top">Ótrúlega fríkuð og flott mynd.  Hissa að hún skildi ekki komast hærra.  14. sæti er samt ekki sem verst.  Til hamingju með það.</td>', 'Ótrúlega fríkuð og flott mynd.  Hissa að hún skildi ekki komast hærra.  14. sæti er samt ekki sem verst.  Til hamingju með það.', '2006-05-01 20:32:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369903, 324139, 20941, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your Blue with this beautiful image!</td>', 'Congratulations on your Blue with this beautiful image!', '2006-05-01 20:49:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369906, 324430, 20941, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your first Ribbon with this outstanding entry! Well deserved and overdue!</td>', 'Congratulations on your first Ribbon with this outstanding entry! Well deserved and overdue!', '2006-05-01 20:50:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(2369911, 324784, 20941, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your ribbon with this stunnung entry!</td>', 'Congratulations on your ribbon with this stunnung entry!', '2006-05-01 20:51:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370094, 324784, 51988, '<td valign="top">Wonderful shot! I love the windmill reflection too!!<br/>Pretty soon, you will have to rent some extra webspace to hold your ribbons! :-)</td>', 'Wonderful shot! I love the windmill reflection too!!Pretty soon, you will have to rent some extra webspace to hold your ribbons! :-)', '2006-05-01 21:21:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370190, 325121, 52336, '<td valign="top">I think we have a softness issue with this image overall, though it could be argued that the interesting mix of elements asks for focus that is not razor-sharp.  There <i>is</i> an interesting mix of elements, with the "new wave" kind of mannequin (hope I don''t sound too old), the ceramic tiled roof that gives a rather old-world feel, and some kind of entrance that looks like it belongs in a modern shopping mall.  I personally like that kind of composition.<br/> <br/>But I think there''s a lack of focus on anything.  Was it intentional, or perhaps was your camera focused on the glass reflections?  The elements that are dark give this image a left-handed weight that detracts for me.  The join in the panes of glass at right are another distracting element.<br/> <br/>This could have been improved, perhaps, by more selecting choosing your subject when shooting, with better focus.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-01 20:59:51.</i></td>', 'I think we have a softness issue with this image overall, though it could be argued that the interesting mix of elements asks for focus that is not razor-sharp.  There is an interesting mix of elements, with the "new wave" kind of mannequin (hope I don''t sound too old), the ceramic tiled roof that gives a rather old-world feel, and some kind of entrance that looks like it belongs in a modern shopping mall.  I personally like that kind of composition. But I think there''s a lack of focus on anything.  Was it intentional, or perhaps was your camera focused on the glass reflections?  The elements that are dark give this image a left-handed weight that detracts for me.  The join in the panes of glass at right are another distracting element. This could have been improved, perhaps, by more selecting choosing your subject when shooting, with better focus.', '2006-05-01 21:58:09.000000', '2006-05-01 21:59:51.000000', 0),
(2370263, 323718, 53668, '<td valign="top">Congratulations!  <br/>I am a huge fan of reflection shots and this one is particularly appealing.</td>', 'Congratulations!  I am a huge fan of reflection shots and this one is particularly appealing.', '2006-05-01 22:17:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370271, 323565, 53668, '<td valign="top">This is beautiful</td>', 'This is beautiful', '2006-05-01 22:20:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370294, 325121, 30982, '<td valign="top">I gave you a 5 and am rather suprised to see it finish as low as it did.  It''s kinda quietly unique.  Met the challenge, yes, but what I think may have hurt your score was the very busy and distracting background.  You note that you shot at 5.6 - not sure if shooting at a wider aperature might have helped or not, but your best bet would probably to have found a different angle of attack if at all possible.  Based on the title, I also found myself trying to find "the man" referenced.  There''s a small face in the corner, but it''s not readily apparent.</td>', 'I gave you a 5 and am rather suprised to see it finish as low as it did.  It''s kinda quietly unique.  Met the challenge, yes, but what I think may have hurt your score was the very busy and distracting background.  You note that you shot at 5.6 - not sure if shooting at a wider aperature might have helped or not, but your best bet would probably to have found a different angle of attack if at all possible.  Based on the title, I also found myself trying to find "the man" referenced.  There''s a small face in the corner, but it''s not readily apparent.', '2006-05-01 22:27:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370296, 324425, 53668, '<td valign="top">Great imaginative story telling photograph...draws me in to examine all the details.  Everything in in character and I love that her make-up is just how they used to wear it in that era.<br/>Well done!</td>', 'Great imaginative story telling photograph...draws me in to examine all the details.  Everything in in character and I love that her make-up is just how they used to wear it in that era.Well done!', '2006-05-01 22:27:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370391, 324657, 53147, '<td valign="top">And once again I come back to this shot. Congartulations on top 10. Well done.</td>', 'And once again I come back to this shot. Congartulations on top 10. Well done.', '2006-05-01 22:59:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370412, 324430, 48954, '<td valign="top">you got it!!! Congrats</td>', 'you got it!!! Congrats', '2006-05-01 23:07:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370537, 324639, 30364, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by BobsterLobster:</b></div><hr/><i> Silhouettes not clear enough. The horizontal line near the centre cuts through boys'' heads and makes photo less effective. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Arggggh!  Two of the things I <i>most</i> liked about it!  The less-than-silhouette treatment established a connection, a small but significant bridge, between the boys and their longed-for outside world; an effect wholly sympathetic to the point of the photograph.  Likewise, the horizontal bar serves a useful figurative purpose, too:  it is a barrier between the two worlds, reinforcing the fact that they are prevented from going outside.   The bar also physically connects the two boys, although this is hardly necessary in appreciating their shared yearning. <br/> <br/>I suppose if your ambition is limited to making a graphically impressive (and technically ''correct'') image, these things might be viewed as flaws; but if you are interested in producing a more durable and more thoughtful photograph, they are worthy inclusions.</td>', 'Originally posted by BobsterLobster: Silhouettes not clear enough. The horizontal line near the centre cuts through boys'' heads and makes photo less effective.   Arggggh!  Two of the things I most liked about it!  The less-than-silhouette treatment established a connection, a small but significant bridge, between the boys and their longed-for outside world; an effect wholly sympathetic to the point of the photograph.  Likewise, the horizontal bar serves a useful figurative purpose, too:  it is a barrier between the two worlds, reinforcing the fact that they are prevented from going outside.   The bar also physically connects the two boys, although this is hardly necessary in appreciating their shared yearning.  I suppose if your ambition is limited to making a graphically impressive (and technically ''correct'') image, these things might be viewed as flaws; but if you are interested in producing a more durable and more thoughtful photograph, they are worthy inclusions.', '2006-05-01 23:51:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370571, 324862, 52336, '<td valign="top">First impression of this photo for me was strong.  I like the bright colours, and the framing matches them well.  The vertical banners present the colours well, breaking up the reflective surface of the windows behind them in a pleasing manner.<br/> <br/>I likely would have cropped the lower portion out, to omit the portion where the window is split horizontally in two.  That area, in my opinion, detracts from the rest of the composition.</td>', 'First impression of this photo for me was strong.  I like the bright colours, and the framing matches them well.  The vertical banners present the colours well, breaking up the reflective surface of the windows behind them in a pleasing manner. I likely would have cropped the lower portion out, to omit the portion where the window is split horizontally in two.  That area, in my opinion, detracts from the rest of the composition.', '2006-05-02 00:08:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370601, 324139, 22734, '<td valign="top">An artist that blooms at his own pace. This work is a steady upward progression of impressive visualization. Both strong and charming. Congratulations on your Blue!</td>', 'An artist that blooms at his own pace. This work is a steady upward progression of impressive visualization. Both strong and charming. Congratulations on your Blue!', '2006-05-02 00:20:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370605, 324430, 22734, '<td valign="top">Quite an artistic study with great expression and color. Congratulations on your RED!</td>', 'Quite an artistic study with great expression and color. Congratulations on your RED!', '2006-05-02 00:22:00.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(2370620, 324784, 22734, '<td valign="top">Possibly the most creative image in this challenge awakening in us that ying-yang sensation of past and future and the interplay within our person. Outstanding and refeshing work. Congratulations on your yellow.</td>', 'Possibly the most creative image in this challenge awakening in us that ying-yang sensation of past and future and the interplay within our person. Outstanding and refeshing work. Congratulations on your yellow.', '2006-05-02 00:25:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370623, 324748, 22734, '<td valign="top">Intriguing and beautiful. Congratulations on your 4th place finish.</td>', 'Intriguing and beautiful. Congratulations on your 4th place finish.', '2006-05-02 00:26:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370627, 324714, 22734, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your 5th place finish with this lovely vista.</td>', 'Congratulations on your 5th place finish with this lovely vista.', '2006-05-02 00:27:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370660, 324430, 39057, '<td valign="top">Very nice. I''m glad you finally made it!<br/> <br/>KS</td>', 'Very nice. I''m glad you finally made it! KS', '2006-05-02 00:34:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370764, 324197, 30982, '<td valign="top">Wow - *way* underrated.  I definitely saw it as the windows framing the room, with the view as a nice added touch.</td>', 'Wow - *way* underrated.  I definitely saw it as the windows framing the room, with the view as a nice added touch.', '2006-05-02 01:16:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370883, 323148, 48228, '<td valign="top">Yes, yes, yes, you did it, as this so deserved this position and score.....Congratulations Brian.....</td>', 'Yes, yes, yes, you did it, as this so deserved this position and score.....Congratulations Brian.....', '2006-05-02 02:25:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370884, 325086, 9387, '<td valign="top">Landcamera-<br/>     I used to submit to challenges but then the stupid people on this site have no idea what really good photography is all about.  This photo is perfect.  The texture in the window frame and the chair is awesome.  It is so simple yet says so much.  As far as I''m concerned this photo of yours was robbed blind.  No way should you have come in 99th.  I would say at least in the top 6 pictures.</td>', 'Landcamera-     I used to submit to challenges but then the stupid people on this site have no idea what really good photography is all about.  This photo is perfect.  The texture in the window frame and the chair is awesome.  It is so simple yet says so much.  As far as I''m concerned this photo of yours was robbed blind.  No way should you have come in 99th.  I would say at least in the top 6 pictures.', '2006-05-02 02:25:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370894, 325090, 48228, '<td valign="top">Great image, great photographer, great result dear friend.....</td>', 'Great image, great photographer, great result dear friend.....', '2006-05-02 02:32:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370933, 324856, 42984, '<td valign="top">It looks like a Robinson R44, four seater helicopter.  The "robbies" are now the biggest selling light planes in the world beating out Cessna, Piper and all the others.</td>', 'It looks like a Robinson R44, four seater helicopter.  The "robbies" are now the biggest selling light planes in the world beating out Cessna, Piper and all the others.', '2006-05-02 02:51:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370941, 324113, 53814, '<td valign="top">I picked this as a top 3. I love the composition (rule of thirds inside a rule of thirds) and your colors give this a sureal "painting" feel. The balance between the outside and inside light is perfect. Not showing the face give this an air of mystery - I think showing his face would have ruined this photo. There are times to break established rules and this is an example. Keep shots like this coming, even if they are not always rewarded with high scores.</td>', 'I picked this as a top 3. I love the composition (rule of thirds inside a rule of thirds) and your colors give this a sureal "painting" feel. The balance between the outside and inside light is perfect. Not showing the face give this an air of mystery - I think showing his face would have ruined this photo. There are times to break established rules and this is an example. Keep shots like this coming, even if they are not always rewarded with high scores.', '2006-05-02 02:55:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2370959, 324430, 47773, '<td valign="top">Beautiful!!!!!!</td>', 'Beautiful!!!!!!', '2006-05-02 03:08:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2371042, 325121, 37858, '<td valign="top">This shot obviously failed (especially without a tripod to help me sharpen the focus - although the thick plate glass window hinders a sharp focus too).  Having said that, this was the intent... I had a polarizer lens on the camera and intentionally left all of the reflections (which most people found distracting) in the image because one of the reflections was the face of "her man" in the lower right corner.  I left the line in the glass in the shot (after experimenting with cropping it out) because I wanted an artificial division between the mannequin and the real man...a sort of contrast between artificial person and real person.  If I had cropped the line out or reduced the reflection to improve the clarity I would have lost the point of the shot.  So I went with this one.  I guess the failing is that it doesn''t work.  I need to rework either the idea or the execution, or both.  I knew I would be hammered for this shot (I have tried something similar with a mannequin before and was scored down) but I still wanted to test the idea.  Nothing ventured, nothing gained...or even learned.  Your comments are appreciated because the learning part is critical for me.  Thanks.</td>', 'This shot obviously failed (especially without a tripod to help me sharpen the focus - although the thick plate glass window hinders a sharp focus too).  Having said that, this was the intent... I had a polarizer lens on the camera and intentionally left all of the reflections (which most people found distracting) in the image because one of the reflections was the face of "her man" in the lower right corner.  I left the line in the glass in the shot (after experimenting with cropping it out) because I wanted an artificial division between the mannequin and the real man...a sort of contrast between artificial person and real person.  If I had cropped the line out or reduced the reflection to improve the clarity I would have lost the point of the shot.  So I went with this one.  I guess the failing is that it doesn''t work.  I need to rework either the idea or the execution, or both.  I knew I would be hammered for this shot (I have tried something similar with a mannequin before and was scored down) but I still wanted to test the idea.  Nothing ventured, nothing gained...or even learned.  Your comments are appreciated because the learning part is critical for me.  Thanks.', '2006-05-02 03:35:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(2371091, 324139, 48717, '<td valign="top">I think I must be going crazy...<br/> <br/>I saw this several times and I continued to think the child was not a child at all, but  one of those teeny tiny delicate porcelain figurines in a small window.  I just saw your forum thread about the picture, and now I see that it is not at all how I kept seeing it.  So, with that said good job at capturing the doll like nature in her.<br/></td>', 'I think I must be going crazy... I saw this several times and I continued to think the child was not a child at all, but  one of those teeny tiny delicate porcelain figurines in a small window.  I just saw your forum thread about the picture, and now I see that it is not at all how I kept seeing it.  So, with that said good job at capturing the doll like nature in her.', '2006-05-02 03:57:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2371231, 324430, 55569, '<td valign="top">beautiful shot, well cropped to highlight the sensitivity of the moment fitting the title!</td>', 'beautiful shot, well cropped to highlight the sensitivity of the moment fitting the title!', '2006-05-02 04:52:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2371287, 324139, 30861, '<td valign="top">I''m reading your comments on this image, and wanted to pop back in to say that "leaving the flowers" is possibly the best single decision you made in this shot :-) I crop the flowers out, and it''s "nice"; I leave them in, and it''s "WOW": they add an unexpected note that''s almost surreal. The more I look at this image the better I like it.</td>', 'I''m reading your comments on this image, and wanted to pop back in to say that "leaving the flowers" is possibly the best single decision you made in this shot :-) I crop the flowers out, and it''s "nice"; I leave them in, and it''s "WOW": they add an unexpected note that''s almost surreal. The more I look at this image the better I like it.', '2006-05-02 05:35:42.000000', NULL, 0),
